{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"de809cff\" lines=\"86-103\">\n<description>\nTurn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with two large foreground colors.\n- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks\n  or protrusions along region boundaries.\n\nRule:\n- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and\n  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.\n- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one\n  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.\n- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).\n\nKey insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding\n3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"edb79dae\" lines=\"39-53\">\n<description>\nStamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.\n- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.\n- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.\n- Stamp each sprite at the placeholder's position inside the frame.\n- Recolor every stamped sprite to the output color defined by the legend.\n\nKey insight: The legend pairs (overlapping two-color glyphs outside the frame) dictate the final recoloring — placeholder color selects the shape, legend maps it to the output color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c7dc4dd\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nLearn a transformation rule from a training pair of framed sprites, then apply it to a test sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid contains several colored square frames (hollow borders of a single color).\n- Each frame's interior holds a small pattern sprite; one frame's interior is blank (all black).\n- Two frames form a training pair (their interiors share the same color palette); one remaining frame is the test case.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the training pair by matching interior color sets; the other non-blank frame is the test input.\n- Infer the rule mapping source→destination (e.g. reflection, color inversion, gap filling, endpoint extraction, or checkerboard complement).\n- Apply that same rule to the test sprite; output the transformed interior.\n\nKey insight: The puzzle is an analogy — \"A is to B as C is to ?\" — where you must discover the visual rule from one example pair and generalize it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\" lines=\"6-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b5ca7ac4\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nMove bordered 5×5 squares to their designated grid edges by border color.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Azure (8) and red (2) bordered 5×5 squares with colored interiors, scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Azure squares → left edge (col 0); red squares → right edge (col W−5).\n- Each square keeps its original row.\n- Process azure left-to-right, red right-to-left (nearest-to-edge first).\n- If a square's rows overlap an already-placed edge square, place it one square-width inward instead.\n\nKey insight: Near-edge-first ordering plus row-overlap determines which square sits at the edge vs. the second column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b9e38dc0\" lines=\"7-18\">\n<description>\nFill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\nRule:\n- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n- Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\nKey insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\" lines=\"42-56\">\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d8e07eb2\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nLook up query glyphs in a reference table and mark whether they form a line.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background with two full-width pink separator rows.\n- Above the first separator is a query row of 5x5 glyph cells.\n- Between separators is a reference table of the same 5x5 cells; a glyph is\n  identified by both its color and its 3x3 shape.\n\nRule:\n- Green-fill the background inside every table cell whose glyph exactly\n  appears in the query row.\n- If the matched table cells form one complete row or one complete column,\n  also green-fill the query band and the bottom verdict band.\n- Otherwise leave the query band unchanged and fill the bottom verdict band red.\n\nKey insight: The bottom color is not a count; it is the yes/no answer to\nwhether the queried glyphs align as a full row or column in the table.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
